### Description

Say you run a request like this one:

```json
{
  "query": {
    "bool": {
      "must": {
        "wildcard": {
          "process.cmd_line": {
            "value": "r*boot",
          }
        }
      },
      "filter": {
        "range": {
          "@timestamp": {
            "gte": "now-1m"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This query has two required clauses that have a high up-front cost: the wildcard query needs to evaluate all matching terms, while the range query need to compute the set of matching documents. Lucene doesn't have a way to choose which one to evaluate first, and it may well end up evaluating the wildcard query before the range filter.

This may not be a bad decision in the general case, however in this case where the range filter is narrow, it's likely that only the recent small segments will match the range filter while older segments will not intersect with the time filter. So we'll be  evaluating the `wildcard` query on all segments, even though a few small segments have matches for the time filter. This is wasteful. This kind of scenario typically happens all the time in alerting use-cases where users run queries every X seconds on the last Y seconds of data.

It would be nice to better optimize this kind of scenario by pruning segments that don't intersect with the time range before we start evaluating the query.
